=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is in router2's routing-protocol subsystem, not the physical WAN interface.
Router2's eth2 (20.20.0.2/8, the wan_r1_r2 link toward router1) is electrically UP with
packets being exchanged — the "unplugged cable" premise in the ticket is incorrect.

The actual root cause is that router2's RIP daemon (ripd) was disabled during last
night's maintenance. The file /etc/frr/daemons has ripd=no, both /etc/frr/frr.conf and
/etc/frr/ripd.conf are empty, and only zebra/mgmtd/staticd are running on the device —
there is no ripd process at all. Because ripd never started (or was killed and not
restarted), router2's routing table contains only its three directly-connected prefixes
(1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8). It has no routes to any of the 192.168.x.x
department networks served by router0 and router1, so every packet from the Server Room
(1.0.0.x) or Internet Lab (128.168.0.x) destined for a department subnet is dropped at
router2 with an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — confirmed by a live ping from pc1
to it_hod. Additionally, router1's stale RIP entries for router2's networks (learned at
lab startup before ripd died) are about to expire (~48 s remaining at time of inspection),
after which the reverse path also disappears.

Corrective fix: On router2, set ripd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons, create a ripd.conf (or
frr.conf) with a router rip block advertising all four attached networks (1.0.0.0/8,
128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8) at version 2, then restart FRR (service frr restart). RIP
will re-converge within one update interval (30 s) and full inter-zone connectivity will
be restored.
=== END ===
